# Chapter 2, part 0: types, working directory, names, checklists, panels.
.h2 File types [file-types]
.ix Document file
.ix Template file
.ix Backup file
.ix Extension of a file
.ix .DOC
.ix .SET
.ix .BAK
.ix Types of file
.ix File extension
The Editor works on three types of file.  All have the Strata document
format (settings followed by text).  The extension of the name and the way a file is made tell
them apart.
.table 10 50
Type | What it is
Document | A file with the extension {c:.doc}.  It holds the text of the
 | document (the body) and all the settings of the document (the header):
 | the global formats, the page layouts, the fonts, the marker formats, the
 | description, the author and the revision.  It is what Save writes.
Template | A file with the extension {c:.set}.  A template is a document that
 | is kept as the pattern for other documents.  It has the settings of a
 | document and, optionally, a starting text.  The New command copies a
 | template; Save with the option Save Global Settings Only makes one.
Backup | A file whose extension is {c:.bak} and a number, such as
 | {c:report.bak01}, in the directory {c:.backup} next to the document.  It is a
 | copy of a document as it was before a Save, kept as many as Backups Kept
 | says.  It can be restored with Restore Backup, or retrieved like any other
 | file.  A file of an older version that is loaded also has a special
 | backup beside it, such as {c:V2-report.doc}; see {n:file-backup-special}.
.end
Two kinds of file that are not documents are written and read by other
menus: a library of cut text (extension {c:.lib}, the Library commands of
the Tools menu) and a set of macros (extension {c:.mac}, the Macros commands
of the Tools menu).  The File commands do not offer them in their checklists.
.note The extension is a convention and not a type.  The type of a file is taken from its content, whatever its name is.  A name such as
  {c:letter.txt} can be saved and retrieved; only the checklists of Save,
  Retrieve and New, which show the files whose names end with the usual
  extensions, do not list it.  The checklists of Delete, Import and Export
  show every file; or type the name in full.
.h3 Document files [file-types-doc]
.ix Document file, content
A document file holds the settings of the document and its text: sections,
blocks and columns.  It also holds the version of the file format
({n:file-versions}).
.h3 Template files [file-types-set]
.ix Template file, content
A template is a full document.  The part of it that matters most is its
settings, because a document that is made from the template takes its global
formats, its page layouts, its fonts and its marker settings from there.
The text of a template is the starting text of the new document, and a
template with no text at all, as Save Global Settings Only makes it, gives a
document that is empty.  The extension {c:.set} is added by Save only when the
option Save Global Settings Only is chosen and the name has no extension.
.h3 Backup files [file-types-bak]
.ix Backup file, content
A backup file is an exact copy of the file that Save replaced.  {X:file-backup} says when it is made and how to use it.  A backup is
distinguished from a document only by its name: a backup that is retrieved
with Retrieve Entire Document becomes the current document under the name of
the backup, so that the next Save writes to the backup file.
.h2 File versions [file-versions]
.ix File versions
.ix Version, file format
.ix File format version
.ix Format version of a file
.ix Older version of a file
.ix Unsupported format
.ix Newer version of a file
Every document, template, library file and macro file carries the version of
its file format.  The version is a whole number: the number that follows the
letter R in the first line of the file, as in {c:*R3}.  The current version is
{b:3}.  A file whose first line is {c:*R2} has the older format that was in
use before Strata, version 2, and is read as it is.  The version of a file
is not the version of the program that wrote it: a document that was made
with any build of the programs has the version of the format that the
build writes.
.h3 What the programs do with a version [file-versions-rule]
.ix File versions, reading and writing
.ix File versions, saving an older file
Every program that reads or writes these files reads the current version and
all older versions, and writes only the current version.  So a file of
version 2 is read, and the next Save writes it as version 3.  No program
lowers the version of a file, because a file that was written in a newer
version can hold what an older version cannot keep.
.table 24 36
Version of the file | What is done
2 | The file is read.  It is written as version 3 when it is saved.
3 | The file is read and written.
Above 3 | The file is not read: see {x:file-versions-errors}.
Below 2, or not a number | The file is not read: see
 | {x:file-versions-errors}.
.end
An older file that is loaded keeps its version on the disk until it is saved;
before that happens a special backup preserves it ({n:file-backup-special}).
.h3 The errors [file-versions-errors]
.ix File versions, errors
.ix Software too old for a file
.ix Not a document of this software
Each program that reads a document reports these errors: the Editor when it
loads a document, a template or a file named in a panel, the Document
Manager in Compare Document Versions and in Restore Backup, and the Printer
when it prints a document.  A file that gives one of them is not changed.
.table 30 30
Message | Meaning and what to do
"The file is version 4.  This | The file was written by newer software
 software reads up to version 3." | than this.  The file is not usable
 | here.  Use a newer version of the
 | software.
"The file version is 1.  This | The version is below 2, or it is
 software reads versions 2 to 3." | damaged (not a number, negative or
 | empty); the message names the value.
 | The format is not supported and the
 | file is not usable by this software.
"The file is not a document of this | The first line of the file is not
 software." | {c:*R} and a number: the file is a text
 | file, a file of another program or a
 | file that is not complete.  It is not
 | a document, and it is not usable here.
.end
.h3 The version of the software [file-versions-software]
.ix File versions, --version
.ix --version, file format
.ix Release, file format version
The option {c:--version} of the programs writes the version of the program
and, after it, the version of the file format that the software writes, such
as {c:file format 3}.  The notes of a release list the same number.  Two
releases with the same file format version write files that each of them
reads; software of a lower version cannot read the files of a higher one.
.h2 Working directory [file-workdir]
.ix Working Directory
.ix Directory, working
.ix Current directory
.ix Working directory, shared
.ix Directory checklist
Every panel of the File menu begins with the field {b:Working Directory}.
It names the directory in which the file of the panel is looked for or
made.  A name that is typed in the next field without a directory is placed
in the working directory; a name that begins with a slash is taken as it is,
and the working directory is then not used.
.h3 How it begins and how it changes [file-workdir-rules]
.ix Working Directory, initial value
.ix Working Directory, shared by panels
The working directory is one value that all the panels share.  The rules are
these.
.table 24 36
Situation | Working Directory
The first File panel of the session | The directory part of the name of the
 | document, if the name was given with a
 | slash (the name {c:notes/plan.doc} gives
 | {c:notes}); otherwise the directory in which
 | the program was started.
A panel accepted with {k:Ins} | The value of the field becomes the new
 | working directory for all the panels,
 | even if the command then fails.
A panel canceled with {k:Esc} | The working directory is not changed.
A checklist of files | Choosing a file in another directory also
 | changes the Working Directory field of
 | the panel (see {n:file-checklist}).
The Macro and Library commands of the | They show the same working directory and
 Tools menu | change it in the same way.
.end
.caution The field is not checked when the cursor leaves it.  A directory that
  does not exist is accepted, and the command that follows fails with a
  message of its own: "cannot write file" for Save, "cannot open file" for
  Retrieve, "Cannot find the file" for Delete.  The directory remains the
  working directory after the failure.  A tilde is not expanded: {c:~/work}
  names a directory called {c:~} in the current place, not the home
  directory.
.h3 Working directory and the Document Manager [file-workdir-docmgr]
.ix Setup, working directory
The Document Manager has a Working Directory of its own on its
Edit / Preview / Print Document panel and in its Setup panel (the field
Initial Working Directory, the setting {c:workdir} of the file
{c:~/.strata/config}).  When the Editor is started, the full name of the
document is given, and the Editor takes the directory part of the name for the
first File panel as the table above says.  A change of the working directory
inside the Editor does not change the one of the Document Manager.
.h2 File names [file-names]
.ix File names, rules
.ix Extension, default
.ix Names with spaces
.ix Long file names
.ix Case of letters in file names
Strata accepts every file name that the system accepts.  No extension is
required.  A name can contain spaces, dots and punctuation, but a name with a
slash is read as a path.  The field in a panel holds up to 511 characters.
.h3 Extensions that the commands add [file-names-ext]
.ix Default extension
.ix Extension added by Save
.ix Extension added by Retrieve
A command adds an extension when the last part of the name (after the last
slash) has no dot in it, or has a dot only as its first character, as
{c:.notes} has.  A name that has a dot anywhere else, even at its end, is left
exactly as it is, and a name may have several dots: the last one starts the
extension.
.table 18 12 29
Command and field | Adds | Rule
Save, Document Name | {c:.doc} | {c:.set} instead if the option is Save
 | {c:.set} | Global Settings Only.
Retrieve, Document Name | {c:.doc} | Only if the name as typed is not a
 | | file: {c:report} is tried as {c:report}
 | | and then as {c:report.doc}.  A name
 | | with dots, such as {c:notes v1.2}, is
 | | tried with {c:.doc} added as well.
New, Document Name | {c:.doc} | Always, when there is no dot.
New, Template | nothing | The name is looked for as typed.
Delete, File Name | nothing | The name must be complete.
Import, ASCII File Name | nothing | The name must be complete.
Export, ASCII File Name | nothing | The name must be complete.
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the Editor started by hand with a name adds nothing to it: the
name {c:report} makes a new document called {c:report}.
.endif
.note Delete does not add {c:.doc}.  For the name {c:report} it shows "Cannot
  find the file" even though {c:report.doc} exists.  Type the full name or
  choose it from the checklist.
.h3 Case of letters [file-names-case]
.ix Upper and lower case in names
.ix File name, case
The file system of the machine determines whether {c:Memo.doc} and {c:memo.doc}
are the same name; on Linux they are two files.  Strata adds to this only two
things.  The name of a template is looked for without regard to the case of
its letters, so that {c:default.set} finds {c:DEFAULT.SET}
({n:file-templates-path}).  And the filters of the checklists and the test for a
backup compare the extension without regard to case, so that a file called
{c:LETTER.DOC} is listed with the documents and gets backups called
{c:LETTER.BAK01} and so on, in the directory {c:.backup}.
.h3 Long names and backups [file-names-long]
.ix Long file names, limit
.ix Backups of a long name
.ix File name, longest
The system does not accept a name of a file that is longer than 255 letters
of the English alphabet, and a letter outside that alphabet counts as two or
three of them.  Strata allows the whole length.  The name of a backup is the
name of the document with {c:.bak01} to {c:.bak99} in place of {c:.doc} (or
after the name, when it does not end in {c:.doc}), so a document whose name
leaves less than six places of room has no backups.  Save accepts such
a document: it is saved, and the message line shows that no backup was made
because the name is too long for a backup.  A name that is longer than the
system allows is refused by Save and by New with the message of the system, and
an output name of more than 255 is rejected by the Printer in the same way.  To
have
backups, keep the name, with its extension, to 249 or fewer.
.h3 Letters outside the English alphabet [file-names-utf8]
.ix UTF-8 in file names
.ix Accented letters in file names
.ix Japanese file names
A name of a file may have any letter that the system accepts.  The field of a
panel that holds a name takes a letter that the keyboard gives, moves over it
as one character, and Backspace and Del remove it whole.  The screen of the
Editor has the characters of the document format, so a letter that is not in
that set (a Chinese or Japanese character, for example) is shown as a question
mark in the lists, the fields and the message line; the file is still found, and
choosing it from a checklist opens that file.  A name that is typed can have
only the letters that the keyboard gives and the screen can show, and a name
inside a document (the file of a Picture or an Include marker) is written in
the characters of the document format, so a file with a name that has
characters outside that set is named in a document only by a document that was
made with that name in it.  The marker Filename prints the letters of the
format that the name has, and a question mark for the others.
.h3 How a long name is shown [file-names-shown]
.ix Long name, how it is shown
.ix Name shortened on the screen
A name that is wider than the room that a line or a list has is shown by its
end, after three dots: {c:...long-name.doc}, because the end of a name and its
extension tell one document from another.  The status line of the Editor
keeps the fields after the name where they are, the message line shortens the
first long name or path in a message, and the lists of names (the checklists,
the list of the documents of a directory) show the end of an entry that is
wider than the list.  A name is never changed on the disk by being shown short.
.h3 Names that look like commands [file-names-signs]
.ix Shell characters in names
.ix Quotes, in file names
.ix Special characters in file names
A name may have spaces, quotes, semicolons, ampersands, dollar signs and
other signs that a command line gives a meaning to.  Strata hands a name to
another program as one piece, never as a line of text for a command
interpreter, so a name such as {c:x; touch y.doc} is a name and nothing
else.  The signs that TeX reads in its own way (percent, hash, dollar,
ampersand, underscore, tilde and caret) are written so that the Printer prints
them as they are, in the text and in the name of a Picture; a name with a brace
or a backslash is refused for a Picture in a file that is written for TeX and
kept in its place ({n:print-export-latex}), and is accepted when the pictures are
copied.
.h3 Directories in a name [file-names-path]
.ix Path in a file name
.ix Absolute path
.ix Relative path
A name with a slash is a path.  If it begins with a slash it is absolute and
the Working Directory field is ignored for that panel (it is still saved as
the working directory).  Otherwise the path is placed under the working
directory: with the working directory {c:/home/ann/work}, the name
{c:drafts/plan.doc} is the file {c:/home/ann/work/drafts/plan.doc}.  The
directory in a path must exist; Save does not make directories.
.h2 Directory and file checklists [file-checklist]
.ix Checklist
.ix F2, checklist
.ix Keypad +, checklist
.ix Alt-+, checklist
.ix Directory list
.ix File list
.ix Use this directory
.ix Selecting a file
.ix Selecting a directory
A checklist is a list of the directories or of the files of a directory;
choosing an entry replaces typing the name.
.h3 How to ask for a checklist [file-checklist-ask]
.ix Checklist, key
Put the cursor on a text field that has a checklist and press {k:F2}, or the
key {k:Keypad +} in the X11 version, or {k:Alt-+} in the terminal version
(the same key as {k:Keypad +}).  The text
fields that have one are the Working Directory field (a list of directories)
and the fields that hold a file name: Document Name, Template, File Name and
ASCII File Name (a list of files).  The fields Description, Author and
Revision have no checklist, and the keys do nothing there.  On a field with
a choice of values (Options, Character Set, Scope) the same keys open the
list of the values, so that {k:F2} always shows the possibilities.  In a
list, {k:Ins} or {k:Enter} takes the entry that is
chosen and {k:Esc} closes the list without a choice; the border of the list
reports this.
.pic pictures/file-checklist-files.png 5 The checklist of files of the Retrieve panel.
.h3 What the lists show [file-checklist-lists]
.ix Checklist of files, contents
.ix Checklist of directories, contents
.ix Hidden files in a checklist
The checklist is a box with the directory as its title; a directory with a
long name is shown by its end, with three dots in front.  It is a menu that
scrolls.
.table 19 41
List | Contents, in this order
Directories (Working Directory) | The entry {c:[Use this directory]}; the
 | entry {c:../} (not at the root of the file
 | system); the subdirectories, each with a
 | slash after the name.
Files (a name field) | The entry {c:../}; the subdirectories, each with
 | a slash after the name; the files whose names
 | end with the extensions of the panel.
.end
Each group is sorted so that names that begin with a capital letter come
before those that begin with a small one.  The files whose names begin with a
dot are listed, like all others.  A directory that cannot be read gives the box
"Directory" with the message "Cannot read the directory".
.pic pictures/file-checklist-dirs.png 5 The checklist of directories of the Retrieve panel.
.h3 Which files each panel shows [file-checklist-filters]
.ix Checklist filter
.ix Wildcards in checklists
The files that a panel lists are chosen by the end of the name, without regard
to case.  The choice belongs to the panel and is as the table shows.
.table 31 29
Field | Shown
Save, Document Name | {c:.doc}, {c:.set} and {c:.bak}
Retrieve, Document Name | {c:.doc}, {c:.set} and {c:.bak}
New, Document Name | {c:.doc}
New, Template | {c:.set}
Delete, File Name | every file
Import, ASCII File Name | every file
Export, ASCII File Name | every file
.end
To see a file with some other ending, for instance a text file to retrieve,
type its whole name, or use Import, Export or Delete whose lists show every
file.
.h3 Keys in a checklist [file-checklist-keys]
.ix Checklist, keys
.ix Menu of choices, keys
The checklist is the same kind of menu that the Editor uses everywhere for a
choice from a list.
.table 17 43
Key | Effect
{k:Up}, {k:Down} | Move the highlight one entry.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The first and the last entry.
{k:PgUp}, {k:PgDn} | Move by a page of the box.
{k:Enter} | Choose the highlighted entry.
{k:Esc} | Close the list and change nothing.
A letter or digit | Move to the next entry that begins with that character
 | (capitals and small letters are the same).  In a list of
 | twelve entries or fewer the entry is chosen at once; in a
 | longer list press {k:Enter} to choose it.
.end
Choosing {c:../} or a name that ends with a slash moves into that directory
and shows its list; the list stays open.  Choosing {c:[Use this directory]}
or a file closes it.  What goes into the panel depends on what was chosen.
.table 26 34
Chosen | Effect on the panel
A directory, in the Working Directory field | The field takes the full path
 | of the directory, with links
 | followed.
A file, in a name field | The name field takes the bare
 | name of the file, and the
 | Working Directory field takes the
 | directory in which it was found.
{k:Esc} | Both fields are left as they were.
.end
.note The directory list shows the directories only, and its entry
  {c:[Use this directory]} is the way to accept the one you are in.  Entering a
  directory is not the same as choosing it: nothing is put in the field until
  you choose {c:[Use this directory]}.
.h2 The File panels: keys and rules [file-panels]
.ix Panel keys
.ix Ins accepts a panel
.ix Esc cancels a panel
.ix Fields of a panel
.ix Text field, editing
.ix Choice field
.ix Replacing the text of a field
All six commands of the menu show a panel: a box with a title, a column of
fields, and at the bottom edge the words "Ins accepts, Esc cancels".  The
keys are the same for all of them, and this section is their one description.
.pic pictures/file-save.png 5 The Save File panel.
.h3 Moving and accepting [file-panels-move]
.ix Next field
.ix Previous field
.table 19 41
Key | Effect
{k:Enter}, {k:Down}, {k:Tab} | Go to the next field.  On the last field
 | nothing happens: {k:Enter} does not accept
 | the panel.
{k:Up}, {k:Shift-Tab} | Go to the previous field.  On the first field
 | nothing happens.
{k:Ins} | Accept the panel: the program checks the fields and,
 | if they are valid, does the command.
{k:Esc} | Cancel the panel at once.  No field is kept, no
 | file is touched and the document is not changed.
.end
.h3 A text field [file-panels-text]
.ix Text field
.ix First key replaces the text
.ix Home, End in a field
When the cursor arrives in a text field the whole text of the field is shown
highlighted.  The first key that you type replaces it, so that to type a new
name you only start typing; the first press of {k:Backspace} or {k:Del} empties the
field.  To keep the text and edit it, press {k:Left}, {k:Right}, {k:Home} or
{k:End} first: the highlight goes and the keys edit the text.
.table 19 41
Key | Effect in a text field
A printable character | Insert it at the cursor (replace the text, if it
 | is the first key).
{k:Left}, {k:Right} | Move the cursor one character.
{k:Home}, {k:End} | The start and the end of the text.
{k:Backspace} | Delete the character before the cursor.
{k:Del} | Delete the character under the cursor.
{k:F2}, {k:Keypad +} | Open the checklist, if the field has one.
.end
A field holds at most 511 characters.  The panel does not scroll the fields
sideways until the text is longer than the box: then the end of the text is
shown.
.h3 A field with choices [file-panels-choice]
.ix Choice field
.ix Options field
.ix Space bar in a choice field
A field that offers a few values (Options, Character Set, Scope) shows the
chosen value in square brackets.  The values are changed in three ways.
.table 13 47
Key | Effect in a choice field
{k:Right} | The next value; on the last value nothing happens.
{k:Left} | The previous value; on the first value nothing happens.
{k:Space} | The next value, and the first one after the last (the
 | values go round).
{k:F2}, {k:Keypad +} | A list of all the values, from which {k:Enter}
 | chooses one and {k:Esc} leaves the value as it was.
.end
.h3 Panels that refuse a value [file-panels-check]
.ix Panel validation
.ix No file name specified
.ix Not a valid revision number
When {k:Ins} is pressed the panel checks its fields.  If a value is not
acceptable a small message box opens, with the title of the panel and the reason,
and the panel stays open with its fields as you left them; press any key to
go on.
.table 31 29
Message | Cause
No file name specified | The name field is empty (Save,
 | Retrieve, New, Delete, Import
 | and Export).
Not a valid revision number | The Revision of Save or New is not
 | digits with at most three decimals.
.end
.h3 Questions and messages [file-panels-yn]
.ix Yes or No question
.ix Press any key
.ix Overwrite old version on disk
.ix Message line
Two other kinds of box appear in the File commands.  A question has the
question as its title and two entries, Yes and No; the highlight starts on
Yes, so that {k:Enter} answers Yes.  Press {k:Y} or {k:N} to answer at once, or
{k:Esc} to answer No.  A message box has a title, one line of text and the
words "Press any key": any key closes it.  After a command has done its work
the Editor writes one line on the message line, above the status line, for
example "Saved" followed by the full name of the file.  It stays until the
next key.
